[1] LEMMA C1-N (PROVED)  M(lambda) terminates  <=>  |lambda| even
    Hand proof: Sum M = w(w+1) + n, so Sum M is even iff n is.  For the converse,
    the Erdos-Gallai inequality holds at EVERY r for M(lambda), with no hypothesis
    beyond lambda_1 = w:  r <= w  gives RHS >= r(r-1)+(w+2-r)r = r(w+1) > rw = LHS;
    r = w+1 gives RHS >= r(r-1) = (w+1)w = LHS; r >= w+2 gives every d_i <= w < r so
    RHS >= r(r-1) >= r(w+1) > rw >= LHS.  (M has >= w+2 entries equal to w, because
    lambda_1 = w is one of them.)  Hence M(lambda) is graphical iff its sum is even,
    and Havel-Hakimi terminates exactly on graphical sequences.
    checked on 4506 partitions n <= 22: parity<=>termination violations 0 ; EG violations 0
   C1-N EG checker can reject a non-graphical list      hits=1      OK
   C1-N parity claim, corrupted to 'always terminates'  hits=388    OK

[2] LEMMA C1-R (PROVED)  ONE descent that subsumes C1-J, C1-K and C1-L's G-descent
    F_s(x,j;nu) := [x]^j u [x-1]^{x+s-j} u nu,  s >= 1, x >= 2, max(nu) <= x-1.
    INTERIOR  1 <= j <= x+1 :   F_s(x,j;nu)  ->  F_s(x-1, j+s-2; nu)
    ENTRY     j = x+s, s >= 2 :  F_s(x,x+s;nu) ->  F_{s-1}(x, s-1; nu)
    Proof (interior): head x; rest = [x]^{j-1} u [x-1]^{x+s-j} u nu of size x+s-1+|nu| >= x.
    Its top x entries are the j-1 copies of x and x-j+1 copies of x-1 (possible since
    s >= 1 leaves x+s-j >= x-j+1), so exactly s-1 copies of x-1 and all of nu stay
    outside; ties with nu-parts equal to x-1 are immaterial, the multiset is the same.
    Decrementing gives [x-1]^{j-1} u [x-2]^{x-j+1}, and the union with the s-1 copies
    left outside is [x-1]^{j+s-2} u [x-2]^{x-j+1} u nu = F_s(x-1, j+s-2; nu).  The block
    holds no 0 because x-1 >= 1.  (Entry): head x, rest = [x]^{x+s-1} u nu, top x are x
    copies of x -> x-1, leaving [x]^{s-1} u nu, i.e. F_{s-1}(x, s-1; nu).   []
    INTERIOR identity verified as a MULTISET on 3644 (s,x,j,nu) instances
    ENTRY    identity verified as a MULTISET on 291 (s,x,nu) instances
   C1-R interior target corrupted to j+s-1 -> matched by process hits=0      OK
   C1-R entry target corrupted to F_{s-1}(x,s) -> matched by process hits=0      OK

    C1-J / C1-K / C1-L's G-descent recovered as INSTANCES of C1-R:
    C1-J as entry+interior: 89 (u,nu) pairs | C1-K as s=2 (j invariant): 81 | C1-L G as s=3: 45

[3] THE BOUNDARY, PRINTED RATHER THAN HIDDEN -- the GENERAL form of C1-P is FALSE
    Tempting general lemma: L terminates, a,b >= 1, L u {a,b} terminates
      ==> residue(L u {a,b}) <= residue(L) + 1  (equivalently steps rises by >= 1).
    UNCONSTRAINED : 324 counterexamples.  minimal: L=(1, 1) + {3,1} : residue 1 -> 3
    a,b <= max(L) : 27 counterexamples.  minimal: L=(5, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1) + {5,1} : residue 4 -> 6
    ==> the [w]^{w+1} head block of M(lambda) is LOAD-BEARING.  C1-P is not an
        instance of any general 'two more entries cost at most one residue' fact,
        and a proof that does not use the head block is proving something false.
   boundary sweep can find a counterexample at all      hits=324    OK
   boundary survives the obvious repair a,b <= max(L)   hits=27     OK

[4] KEY LEMMA C1-P (CENSUS -- NOT PROVED)
    lambda has k >= 4 parts, |lambda| even; i<j in {2..k} with lambda_i = lambda_j mod 2;
    lambda^- := lambda minus those two parts.  Then
        residue(M(lambda))  <=  residue(M(lambda^-)) + 1.
    (lambda^-_1 = lambda_1, |lambda^-| even, so C1-N makes M(lambda^-) terminate: the
     statement never has an undefined right-hand side.)
