[5] THEOREM C1-O (PROVED reduction)   C1-N + Thm C1-2 + Thm C1-3 + C1-P  ==>  C1-M
    Induction on k.  k=2: Theorem C1-2 gives residue = 2 = bound(2).  k=3: Theorem C1-3
    gives residue in {2,3}, and bound(3) = 3.  k >= 4: pick the pigeonhole pair, apply
    C1-P and the hypothesis at k-2:  residue <= bound(k-2) + 1 = ceil((k-2)/2)+2 =
    ceil(k/2)+1 = bound(k).   []   -- the arithmetic step is checked below.
    bound(k-2)+1 == bound(k) for every k in 4..199 : YES
    C1-M ==> C1 (residue != k+1) : ceil(k/2)+1 < k+1 for k >= 2 : True
    C1-M ==> C1-I's open half (no near-miss at k >= 4, i.e. residue <= k-1) : [4, 5, 6, 7] ... first k is 4

